answer:
Ogham is not automatically handled by CSS Writing Modes’ vertical mixed typesetting. The CSS specification recommends using sideways text orientation for Ogham.

For a bottom-to-top Ogham inscription:

```css
.ogham {
  writing-mode: vertical-lr;
  text-orientation: sideways-left;
}
```

This makes the text run bottom to top. In newer CSS Writing Modes terminology, the equivalent is:

```css
.ogham {
  writing-mode: sideways-lr;
}
```

Ogham’s Unicode glyphs have horizontal orientation, so they need to be rotated sideways rather than treated as intrinsically vertical. CSS `writing-mode` is preferable to `transform: rotate(...)` because it affects layout dimensions and flow, not merely the rendered graphics.
